t with F2 pung it's 2 points, still below 8). I may need to aim for a higher pattern, but with many tiles gone, high patterns are unlikely.  \n\nGiven the situation, I should aim to win quickly if possible, but must ensure at least 8 points. However, in practice, maybe the hand can be worth 8 with combinations: Prevalent Wind (2) + Seat Wind (if I had F1 pung, but I don't) + maybe Dragon Pung (none) + All Chows (2) + Self Drawn (1) + Edge Wait (1) + Closed Wait (1) etc. But Edge/Closed/Single waits are mutually exclusive. Let's estimate: if I complete with a chow and pair, I could have All Chows (2) + Prevalent Wind (2) = 4, plus maybe Self Drawn (1) = 5, still short. Possibly Pung of Terminals or Honors (1) = 6. Still short.  \n\nI might need to change hand structure: perhaps try to get a pung or kong for more points. But with few tiles left, difficult.  \n\nConsidering discards:  \n- Discard T5: keeps T7,T8 (potential chow with T6/T9) and W8, B2.  \n- Discard T8: keeps T5,T7 (no sequence) and W8,B2.  \n- Discard T7: keeps T5,T8 (need T6/T7/T9? Not a sequence)  \n- Discard B2: keeps all Bamboo tiles + W8.  \n- Discard W8: keeps all Bamboo + B2.  \n\nGiven I have two chows in Bamboo (T3-T5) and Dots (B7-B9), perhaps aiming for Half Flush (6 points) if I can get only Bamboo and honors. My honors are F2, so Half Flush requires only Bamboo and honors. Currently I have Bamboo tiles T5,T7,T8 and W8 (Character) and B2 (Dot). If I discard W8 and B2, I become all Bamboo + honors. But I need to complete hand with Bamboo tiles. That could be promising: Half Flush (6) + Prevalent Wind (2) + maybe All Chows (2) if all melds are chows = 10 points, enough. But my exposed melds include Dots chow (B7-B9) and F2 pung (honor), so Half Flush requires all tiles to be from one suit or honors. The Dots chow violates Half Flush because it's not Bamboo or honor. So Half Flush impossible.  \n\nAnother possibility: All Types (6 points) requires all three suits + winds + dragons. I already have winds (F2), but no dragons yet. Could try to get a dragon pung, but dragons are scarce (J1:2 left, J2:2 left, J3:0 left). Unlikely.  \n\nGiven the low chance of high points, maybe I should just try to win quickly and hope for enough small patterns. But must ensure at least 8. Let's check possible patterns if I win with a chow and pair:  \nPatterns:  \n- Prevalent Wind (2)  \n- All Chows (2)  \n- Pung of Terminals or Honors (1) for F2  \n- Self Drawn (1)  \n- Edge Wait (1) if waiting on 3 or 7  \n- Closed Wait (1) if middle tile  \n- Single Wait (1) if pair wait  \nBut Edge/Closed/Single are exclusive, so max one of them. That totals max: 2+2+1+1+1 = 7, still 7 points. Need one more. Maybe Two Terminal Chows (1 point) if I have chows 123 and 789 in same suit? My melds: T3-T5 is 345, not terminal chow. B7-B9 is terminal chow (789). So I have one terminal chow. If I can get another terminal chow (123) in same suit? Possibly if I form chow T1-T2-T3, but T1,T2,T3 are mostly used. Unlikely.  \nOr Pure Double Chow (1 point) if I have two identical chows. Not currently.  \nThus, it seems difficult to reach 8 points with current hand.  \n\nMaybe I should consider changing hand to try for Seven Pairs (24 points) which is high enough. Seven Pairs requires 7 pairs, all concealed. My exposed melds would disqualify because they are not pairs. So Seven Pairs impossible.  \n\nGiven the constraints, perhaps the best is to discard tiles that improve flexibility and hope to draw into a better configuration. Since I need at least 8 points, I might need to aim for a hand like All Pungs (6) + Prevalent Wind (2) + maybe Dragon Pung (2) etc. But with current hand, all pungs unlikely.  \n\nObservation: many tiles are exhausted, making it hard to complete sequences. T6 (1 left) and T9 (0 left) are scarce. T9 is gone, so only T6 available for chow with T7,T8. So T6 is the only tile to complete chow T7,T8. B2 has 3 left, W8 has 2 left, T5 has 2 left.  \n\nIf I aim for a pair wait with chow 