raw B2 \u2192 hand: T5, T7, B2, B2, W8 \u2192 pair B2, left T5, T7, W8 \u2192 no meld.  \n- Draw W8 \u2192 pair W8, left T5, T7, B2 \u2192 no meld.  \n- Draw T5 \u2192 pair T5, left T7, B2, W8 \u2192 no meld.  \nSo still not tenpai.  \n\nGiven the complexity, perhaps the best is to discard the most isolated tile, W8, and then hope to draw into a better shape. But let's check discarding B2:  \nHand: T5, T7, T8, W8.  \nPotential:  \n- Draw T6 \u2192 T5, T6, T7, T8, W8 \u2192 chow T6,T7,T8, left T5, W8 \u2192 need pair.  \n- Draw T5 \u2192 pair T5, left T7,T8,W8 \u2192 no meld.  \n- Draw W8 \u2192 pair W8, left T5,T7,T8 \u2192 no meld.  \nNot tenpai.  \n\nDiscarding T5:  \nHand: T7, T8, B2, W8.  \n- Draw T6 \u2192 T6,T7,T8 chow, left B2, W8 \u2192 need pair.  \n- Draw B2 \u2192 pair B2, left T7,T8,W8 \u2192 no meld.  \n- Draw W8 \u2192 pair W8, left T7,T8,B2 \u2192 no meld.  \nNot tenpai.  \n\nSo none of the discards put me in tenpai immediately. I need to think about future draws.  \n\nGiven the game state, many high-value tiles are gone, and opponents have many melds, so they might be close to winning. I should prioritize defense? But I don't have enough information to defend strongly.  \n\nConsidering the history, I have not discarded many dangerous tiles recently.  \n\nI think the best is to discard W8 because it is less connected and has fewer copies for opponents to use. Also, W8 is safe based on discard history: previous player played W7, W4; next player played W1, W3; across played W8, W9, W5. W8 was discarded by across once, and not recently. So relatively safe.  \n\nSituation: Hand is not in tenpai, needs improvement. Points are low, but we must try to win. Discard W8 to keep Bamboo sequence potential.  \n\nEvaluation:  \n- Discard W8 reduces suit diversity and keeps T7,T8 for chow with T6.  \n- B2 and T5 are kept for pairing.  \n- Safe discard relative to opponents' discards.  \n\nReason: W8 is isolated with no sequence potential, and keeping Bamboo tiles offers more flexibility. B2 has more copies for potential pair. T5 could pair or form chow with drawn T4 or T6.  \n\nAnswer: Play W8.  \n\nSituation: Not in tenpai, hand has low scoring potential, need to improve toward winning while considering safety.  \nEvaluation: Discarding W8 is relatively safe and keeps hand flexible with T7,T8 chow possibility.  \nReason: W8 has no sequence potential given remaining tiles, and opponents have not shown recent interest in W8. Keeping B2 and T5 offers better pairing chances.  \nAnswer: Play W8"}
